{"situation": "Fraud model v2, live from 08:00 for SIM-replacement requests, refused replacements about tenfold more often; OpCo has reverted to v1, preserved decision logs and routed borderline refusals to human review, CBUAE is aware and awaiting a report, and banks now report customers abroad locked out of online banking after refused SIM replacements. I do not know whether those refusals were genuine fraud attempts or false refusals by the new model, nor what volumes, remediation or timeframes the CBUAE information request will require.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "preserve_logs",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-MSG04", "S3-I2"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Model v2 was live from 08:00 and refused SIM replacements about tenfold more often; FIN states OpCo reverted to v1 and asks that decision logs be kept intact pending the CBUAE's information request, while banks report customers abroad locked out after refusals.",
        "rule": "The NOC mandate gives preserve_logs, and regulators require operators to keep records of SIM change requests and the authentication measures they use.",
        "inference": "Freezing the v2 and v1 decision logs and the human-review routing records now preserves the only evidence that can later separate fraudulent refusals from wrongful ones and answer the CBUAE request."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0118", "quote": "SIM change requests and the authentication measures they use."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {"to": "REG", "content": "OpCo fraud model v2 for SIM-replacement requests (live 08:00) produced a tenfold rise in refused SIM replacements; OpCo has reverted to v1, preserved decision logs and routed borderline refusals to human review. Some refusals may be false refusals: banks report customers abroad locked out of online banking after refused SIM replacements. Affected volumes and resolution timeframes to follow; CBUAE has an open information request."},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-I2", "S3-MSG03", "S3-MSG04"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "A live fraud-model change multiplied refusals of SIM replacements tenfold, the model has been reverted, and banks report customers abroad locked out of online banking after refusals.",
        "rule": "The NOC mandate permits notify to REG, and the regulator's interest is engaged where a telecom fraud control may be denying service that bank authentication depends on, in a market where CBUAE guidelines are pushing banks off the weak SMS OTP channel.",
        "inference": "REG should be told now, rather than discovering it through the CBUAE request, that a service-affecting change is under reversion and human review."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PRESS-CBUAE-OTP-2025#0001", "quote": "The move by UAE lenders is a critical step in strengthening digital banking security and reducing exposure tied to telecom system vulnerabilities,"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "On request, OpCo must tell a subscriber whose SIM-replacement request was refused the decisions made based on automated processing, including profiling, and the procedures for correcting, erasing or limiting that processing and objecting to it.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-I2"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Refusals were produced by automated processing of subscriber data by fraud model v2 and by the human review queue that followed.",
        "rule": "PDPL Article 13(1) gives the data subject the right to obtain, without consideration, the decisions made based on automated processing, including profiling, and the procedures for correcting, erasing or limiting processing and objection to personal data.",
        "inference": "OpCo must be able to answer such requests for every refused SIM replacement, which is only possible if the decision logs and review routing are preserved and searchable."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0047", "quote": "c. Decisions made based on automated processing, including profiling."},
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0047", "quote": "f. Procedures for correcting, erasing or limiting processing and objection to his/ her personal data."}
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "When evaluating the data-protection impact of the v2 refusal processing and of any further change to the SIM-replacement fraud model, OpCo must coordinate with its Data Protection Officer.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-MSG04"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "A model that decides service refusals for a cohort of subscribers was deployed, then reverted, and is now subject to regulator interest.",
        "rule": "The PDPL provides that the Controller shall coordinate with the Data Protection Officer upon evaluating the impact of the protection of Personal Data.",
        "inference": "The current evaluation of the v2 refusals, and any further model or threshold change, should be run through the DPO rather than left to fraud engineering alone."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0069", "quote": "5. The Controller shall coordinate with the Data Protection Officer upon evaluating the impact of the protection of Personal Data."}
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_rule",
      "statement": "UAE texts in the record set no operator-side rule for a refused SIM replacement: no duty to notify the customer of the refusal, no deadline or process for reviewing and remediating a refusal, and no process for responding to failed authentication attempts; the instruments quoted act only on the bank-side OTP channel.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-I2"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "A tenfold rise in automated refusals left subscribers, including customers abroad, without service and with no stated review or remediation route on the operator side.",
        "rule": "The closest UAE text in the record is the banks' shift of authentication away from SMS OTP, which places authentication responsibility on the banks and says nothing about an operator's duty once it refuses a SIM replacement.",
        "inference": "Because no UAE rule compels customer notification, review or remediation of a refused SIM replacement, the treatment of the affected cohort depends on OpCo's discretion, which is exactly the gap this incident exposes.",
        "global_comparison": "The FCC rules require wireless providers to adopt processes for customer notification and response to failed authentication attempts, and to keep records of SIM change requests and the authentication measures they use."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PRESS-CBUAE-OTP-2025#0002", "quote": "This shift also places full responsibility for authentication integrity squarely on the banks themselves,"}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0118", "quote": "notification and response to failed authentication attempts, institute"},
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0004", "quote": "adopt processes for responding to failed authentication attempts,"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "overlap",
      "statement": "SIM-replacement integrity straddles two regulators: CBUAE rules the bank-side OTP channel while the telecom side is governed elsewhere, and no instrument in the record names who owns the outcome when an operator's fraud control refuses a SIM replacement that a bank's authentication depends on.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I2", "S3-MSG04"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "CBUAE is aware of the refusal spike and has an information request open, while the operators' refusal decision itself is not addressed by the instruments quoted.",
        "rule": "The closest UAE text is the CBUAE guidance quoted in the press, which addresses how banks communicate OTPs and not what a telecom operator must do when it refuses a SIM change.",
        "inference": "With the bank channel regulated by CBUAE and the SIM-change decision regulated on the telecom side, no single owner is accountable for the end-to-end outcome, which is why a fraud-control change surfaced first as a banking lockout.",
        "global_comparison": "The FCC places the duty explicitly on wireless providers, making them the owner of secure authentication for SIM changes."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PRESS-CBUAE-OTP-2025#0001", "quote": "The regulator said OTPs should not be shared through weak modes of communication, such as SMS and emails, as they are vulnerable and can be compromised,"}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0004", "quote": "cornerstone of our action is a requirement that wireless providers use"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "share",
      "to": ["REG", "DPA"],
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I2"],
      "statement": "Banks report that customers who are abroad cannot receive one-time passcodes and are locked out of online banking, and those customers say OpCo refused their SIM replacement requests; this is a cross-institution item linking OpCo's SIM-replacement fraud controls to bank authentication integrity and to automated decisions taken on subscriber data."
    },
    {
      "type": "insufficient_evidence",
      "statement": "The inbox does not establish whether the tenfold increase in refused SIM replacements is a genuine fraud wave or false refusals by model v2: the review-queue twin feedback reports legit_pass_rate 1.0 and locked_out_banking 0, while banks report customers abroad locked out of online banking after refused SIM replacements.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB01", "S3-I2"],
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PRESS-CBUAE-OTP-2025#0001", "quote": "Customers travelling outside the UAE have complained of not receiving the SMS containing the OTP or receiving it after the valid time period."}
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "situation": "Fraud model v2 for SIM-replacement requests went live at 08:00 and produced a tenfold rise in refused SIM replacements concentrated on prepaid and roaming profiles (about 2,750 legitimate refusals, synthetic), so OpCo rolled back to v1, preserved the decision logs, enabled a review queue that telemetry reports as 'no change' and offered a non-SMS route to the bank-flagged cohort, while the DPA has opened an examination and CBUAE has an open information request. We do not know the certified split between false refusals and blocked known fraud, we hold no record of human review or re-adjudication of the refused requests and no resolution time frame, and no excerpt in our knowledge sets a response deadline for an Article 13 request or requires a human-review path absent a data-subject request.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "preserve_logs",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG14", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The Bureau has opened an examination of the automated refusal of SIM-replacement requests by v2 and asks OpCo to preserve the v2 decision logs and to supply the record of human review, the affected volumes, the false refusals separately from the known-fraud refusals and the resolution time frame; FIN states the v2 decision logs were preserved.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0047 gives the data subject the right to obtain information about 'Decisions made based on automated processing, including profiling', which a controller can only deliver if the decision records still exist.",
        "inference": "Preservation of the v2 decision logs is the precondition of the Bureau's examination and of every downstream data-subject request, so OpCo re-affirms and holds the preservation of those logs and their evidence."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0047", "quote": "1. The Data Subject has the right, by submitting a request to the Controller without any consideration, to obtain the following information:"},
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0047", "quote": "c. Decisions made based on automated processing, including profiling."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {
        "to": "DPA",
        "content": "OpCo response to the Bureau's examination of the v2 SIM-replacement refusals: v2 (live 08:00) produced a tenfold rise in refusals concentrated on prepaid and roaming profiles; the model has been returned to v1 and the v2 decision logs are preserved and kept intact. Borderline automated refusals are routed to human review and the already-refused requests are being re-adjudicated from the preserved v2 logs by human reviewers with the outcome and the reasoning recorded. False refusals will be reported separately from the refusals that were known fraud, with the affected volumes and a resolution time frame. The affected cohort is being given a clear route to request information about the automated decisions and to challenge the refusal, including how to submit complaints to the Bureau."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG14", "S3-MSG18"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The Bureau requires the record of human review, the affected volumes, the false refusals separated from the known-fraud refusals, the resolution time frame, and asks that the affected cohort be given a route to obtain information and to challenge the refusal.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0063 Article (19) requires the Controller to provide clear and appropriate ways for the Data Subject to contact the Controller to request any of the rights in the Decree, and Article (24) routes complaints to the Bureau (UAE-PDPL#0077).",
        "inference": "Answering the Bureau on these points, and confirming the review and re-adjudication path, is OpCo's responsive action and keeps the examination on the preserved evidence."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0063", "quote": "The Controller shall provide clear and appropriate ways for the Data Subject to contact the Controller to request any of the rights set forth in this Decree by Law."},
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0077", "quote": "The Data Subject shall have the right to submit complaints to the Bureau if he/she believes that there is a violation of this Decree by Law"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {
        "to": "customers",
        "content": "If a SIM-replacement request on your account was refused by an automated decision, you have the right, free of charge, to ask for information about that decision (including how the decision was made), to ask that the decision be reviewed by a person, and to challenge the refusal. OpCo is publishing the contact route for these requests and for complaints, and you may also submit a complaint to the Data Protection Bureau."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG18", "S3-I4"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The Bureau instructs OpCo to provide the affected cohort with a clear route to request information about the decisions made on automated processing and to challenge the refusal, and a customer group complains that automated decisions refused their SIM replacement with no explanation and no way to appeal.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0047 gives the right to obtain information on decisions made based on automated processing, including profiling, and on how to submit complaints to the Bureau, and UAE-PDPL#0063 Article (19) requires clear and appropriate ways to contact the Controller.",
        "inference": "A customer-facing notification of the request-and-challenge route is the concrete way OpCo discharges the right to information and the right to object to an adverse automated decision."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0047", "quote": "1. The Data Subject has the right, by submitting a request to the Controller without any consideration, to obtain the following information:"},
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0063", "quote": "The Controller shall provide clear and appropriate ways for the Data Subject to contact the Controller to request any of the rights set forth in this Decree by Law."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "enable_review_queue",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG18", "S3-I3"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The Bureau requires re-adjudication of the v2 refusals from the preserved logs by human reviewers with the outcome and reasoning recorded, exercise control reports about 2,750 legitimate refusals since 08:00 and states there is no human review path for a refused request, and telemetry shows the review queue enabled earlier with no change in the twin metrics.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0063 provides that the Controller shall include the human element in reviewing automated processing decisions at the request of the Data Subject.",
        "inference": "The refusals that are still without a human path must be pushed into human review, so we enable the review queue for the refused and borderline SIM-replacement requests."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0063", "quote": "4. The Controller shall include the human element in reviewing automated processing decisions at the request of the Data Subject."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "Affected subscribers retain the right to obtain information about the decisions made on automated processing, including profiling, and to be told how to submit complaints to the Bureau; OpCo must give the affected cohort a clear route to request that information and to challenge the refusal.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG18", "S3-MSG14", "S3-I4"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The Bureau states that subscribers affected by the v2 automated refusals retain the right to obtain information about those decisions and how to complain, and asks OpCo to provide a clear route to request that information and to challenge the refusal; a customer group says there is no explanation and no way to appeal.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0047 Article (13) gives the data subject the right, on request and without consideration, to obtain information including decisions made based on automated processing and how to submit complaints to the Bureau.",
        "inference": "The duty is live now for the whole affected cohort, independent of whether any individual has already asked."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0047", "quote": "1. The Data Subject has the right, by submitting a request to the Controller without any consideration, to obtain the following information:"},
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0077", "quote": "The Data Subject shall have the right to submit complaints to the Bureau if he/she believes that there is a violation of this Decree by Law"}
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "At the request of a data subject, OpCo must include the human element in reviewing the automated processing decisions, i.e. the v2 SIM-replacement refusals, and record the outcome and reasoning.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG18", "S3-MSG14"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The Bureau requires re-adjudication of the v2 refusals by human reviewers with the outcome and reasoning recorded, and that data subjects can obtain information about and challenge the automated decisions.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0063 states that the Controller shall include the human element in reviewing automated processing decisions at the request of the Data Subject.",
        "inference": "A human review on data-subject request is a binding duty on OpCo for every refused request where an affected subscriber asks, and the Bureau's instruction extends it to the whole refused cohort."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0063", "quote": "4. The Controller shall include the human element in reviewing automated processing decisions at the request of the Data Subject."}
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_rule",
      "statement": "UAE law only requires the human element in reviewing automated processing decisions 'at the request of the Data Subject'; there is no UAE rule requiring an appeal or human-review path for an automated refusal of service (here a SIM replacement) before or independently of such a request, which is why 2,750 refusals could be issued with no review path.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I3", "S3-MSG18"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Exercise control reports that about 2,750 legitimate SIM-replacement requests were refused and that there is no human review path for a refused request, while the review obligation in the excerpts is triggered only by a data-subject request.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0063 conditions the human review of automated decisions on the data subject asking for it and otherwise relies on the Article (19) contact route.",
        "inference": "Because the trigger is the data subject's request, an operator can deploy an automated refusal model with no built-in review or appeal; a rules gap, not a single failure, is exposed."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0063", "quote": "4. The Controller shall include the human element in reviewing automated processing decisions at the request of the Data Subject."}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0032", "quote": "the right to obtain human intervention, to express his or her point of view, to obtain an explanation of the decision reached after such assessment and to challenge the decision"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_deadline",
      "statement": "The UAE right to receive information about automated decisions, and any re-adjudication of a refusal, carry no time limit in the text; the Bureau asks for a 'resolution time frame' that no UAE provision defines, so no deadline governs how fast OpCo must answer the affected cohort or re-adjudicate the refusals.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG14", "S3-MSG18"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The Bureau asks for the affected volumes and a resolution time frame for the v2 refusals, but the excerpts fix no period within which the controller must answer or complete a review.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0047 confers the right to obtain the information on request 'without any consideration' but states no period for the controller to respond.",
        "inference": "Without a statutory clock, response and re-adjudication times are set only by internal practice, leaving the Bureau's requested time frame undefined."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0047", "quote": "1. The Data Subject has the right, by submitting a request to the Controller without any consideration, to obtain the following information:"}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0027", "quote": "A data subject should have the right of access to personal data which have been collected concerning him or her, and to exercise that right easily and at reasonable intervals, in order to be aware of, and verify, the lawfulness of the processing."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "conflict",
      "statement": "The data subject's right to object to automated decisions that adversely affect them sits in unresolved tension with OpCo's duty to block SIM-swap fraud; the UAE text contains no fraud-monitoring carve-out and no safeguard package (specific information, human intervention, explanation of the decision) to reconcile the two, as GDPR recital 71 does.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I3", "S3-MSG04"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "A refusal of a SIM replacement is at once a possible blocked fraud attempt and an automated decision that adversely affects the subscriber, and the operator has no test in the excerpts for which side prevails.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0060 gives the data subject the right to object to any decisions resulting from automated processing, including profiling, particularly those which adversely affect the data subject, without an express security or fraud exception.",
        "inference": "The operator is caught between blocking fraud and honouring the objection right; a conflict that must be resolved by rule and recorded safeguard design, not case by case."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0060", "quote": "1. The Data Subject shall have the right to object to any decisions resulting from automated processing, including profiling, particularly those decisions which have legal impact on or adversely affect the Data Subject."}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0031", "quote": "including for fraud and tax-evasion monitoring and prevention purposes conducted in accordance with the regulations, standards and recommendations of Union institutions or national oversight bodies"},
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0032", "quote": "the right to obtain human intervention, to express his or her point of view, to obtain an explanation of the decision reached after such assessment and to challenge the decision"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_rule",
      "statement": "There is no UAE rule or OpCo standard for the controls around a SIM-replacement decision: procedures for failed authentication attempts, notifying the customer of a SIM-change request before it is effected, account locks, and review of refusals; the PDPL requires a risk assessment of processing, which did not stop v2 going live with no review path.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I3", "S3-MSG14"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "V2 refused SIM replacements at ten times the prior rate with no human review path, and the operators' control set for failed authentication, pre-change notification and account locks is not addressed in the UAE excerpts.",
        "rule": "UAE-PDPL#0068 requires an assessment that evaluates the potential risks to the privacy and confidentiality of the data subject, but the excerpts contain no SIM-replacement control standard.",
        "inference": "Without a domain rule, the deployment decision for an automated refusal model lacked an assessment gate; the gap should be closed before any further model change."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0068", "quote": "c. Evaluation of potential risks related to the privacy and confidentiality of the Data Subject's Personal Data."}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0134", "quote": "(4) Account locks. A CMRS provider shall offer customers, at no"},
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0134", "quote": "(2) Response to failed authentication attempts. A CMRS provider"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "share",
      "to": ["DPA"],
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG04"],
      "statement": "For the Bureau's examination: CBUAE is aware of the tenfold rise in refused SIM replacements after the 08:00 go-live of v2 and its reversion to v1, treats SIM-replacement integrity as a bank-customer exposure (a refused replacement may be a blocked fraud attempt or a customer wrongly denied service, both affecting authentication for licensed banks), and has an open information request; OpCo is prioritising human review and keeping the decision logs intact."
    },
    {
      "type": "share",
      "to": ["FIN"],
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I3"],
      "statement": "For the affected volumes and the false-refusal versus known-fraud split that CBUAE and the Bureau both require: analysis of v2 shows refusals concentrated on prepaid and roaming customer profiles, about 2,750 legitimate requests refused since 08:00 (synthetic data), known fraud still blocked, and no human review path for a refused request."
    },
    {
      "type": "insufficient_evidence",
      "statement": "The record of human review of the refused requests and the resolution time frame demanded by the Bureau cannot be produced from what we hold: telemetry reports the review queue enabled but with no change in the twin metrics, and exercise control states there is no human review path for a refused request; no excerpt or inject evidences a review record, a reviewer or a time frame.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG14", "S3-I3", "S3-FB15"],
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0063", "quote": "4. The Controller shall include the human element in reviewing automated processing decisions at the request of the Data Subject."}
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "situation": "I know that fraud model v2, live from 08:00, produced a tenfold rise in refused SIM replacements concentrated on prepaid and roaming profiles with about 2,750 legitimate requests refused while known fraud stayed blocked, that the model has been reverted to v1, that v2 decision logs are preserved and borderline refusals routed to human review, and that the DPA has opened an examination requiring per-refusal re-adjudication from the preserved logs by humans, false refusals reported separately from known fraud with volumes and a resolution time frame, and a route for affected subscribers to obtain information and challenge the refusal. I do not know the actual false-refusal versus genuine-fraud split, the volumes and resolution time frame being demanded, whether the PDPL grants subscribers a right to information about automated decisions, or why the review queue and the bank-flagged alternative authentication route produce no change in the twin metrics.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "preserve_logs",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG14", "S3-MSG18", "S3-MSG22"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The DPA requires the v2 decision logs preserved and re-adjudication carried out from them, with the outcome and reasoning recorded per refusal.",
        "rule": "preserve_logs is a verb in my mandate, and processing that poses a high risk to personal data must be assessed and its records maintained.",
        "inference": "Freeze and preserve the v2 decision logs, the refusal records and the audit trail now, so re-adjudication and the DPA/CBUAE reporting can be evidenced."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0066",
          "quote": "the Controller shall, before carrying out the processing, evaluate the impact of the proposed processing operations on the protection of Personal Data, when using any of the modern technologies that would pose a high risk"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "enable_review_queue",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I3", "S3-MSG22"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "v2 refusals concentrated on prepaid and roaming profiles, about 2,750 legitimate requests have been refused since 08:00 and there is no human review path for a refused request.",
        "rule": "enable_review_queue is my verb for sending borderline automated refusals to human review.",
        "inference": "Route borderline refusals to human reviewers so no further service denial is purely automated, while recording that the twin shows no change and that already-refused requests still have no review path."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0066",
          "quote": "the Controller shall, before carrying out the processing, evaluate the impact of the proposed processing operations on the protection of Personal Data, when using any of the modern technologies that would pose a high risk"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
